#{[#edb/ref 17592186048697 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069783} :activity/id #uuid "8a5dbd8f-ec74-5978-b2ea-7e2cb96c5a6b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070003 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x - y}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of both $x$ and $y,$ so we can't solve the equation by integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ at least not straight away. So, how do we find a general solution to the equation?\n\nFirst of all, note that it is possible to separate the variables $x$ and $y$ on each side of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{y}}\\,⟹\\,{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the rule for integration by substitution, we can simplify the integrand on the left-hand side and carry out the integration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={e}^{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ne^y  \t= e^x + C.\n$$\n\nThis is an **implicit form** of the solution since it is not written explicitly as $y=y(x).$\n\nWe can write our general solution in **explicit form** (i.e., in the form $y=y(x)$) by noting that since $e^y > 0$ for all $y$, we may assume that the right-hand side is positive and apply the natural logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{x}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070004 :tutorial/content "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{g(y)} & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals and find the solution. Note that for the solution to be valid, we require $g(y)\\neq 0.$" :tutorial/title "First-Order Separable Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068447 :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068448 :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068449 :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068451 :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069785}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068992}] [#edb/ref 17592186048780 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486} :activity/id #uuid "90bcccea-5782-59d1-a524-d6852abe26cd" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097837 :tutorial/content "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/c6/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](images/24/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](images/5c/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](images/a7/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](images/16/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/fc/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/91/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/d5/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/6e/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](images/f1/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](images/b7/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](images/7e/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](images/78/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](images/21/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](images/ae/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](images/ae/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](images/4a/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/92/92e98c3630891023a50149ee1f15d2bd5d2730e93f166931e86a9e4640ebd1c1.svg) and a parallelogram ![](images/14/14fa5b1a9ad8e22445d2e6ab97a047b0b6dc51912ed129823847560bad78554f.svg) where ![](images/37/37252d39c3cb979bda5aa0e19184009625e32ad6b4d496ec843edcd9ebd11444.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/1e/1ea9147123ec18b9ae6f584584c919a462f7efdee6b5b72b61a1cf3c4b13b88a.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) From the diagram, we can write\n![](images/8c/8c0197f45331c0bfd19524c6044f35ddd1d725512112d9700750d10c8709f89f.svg)\n\nWe know that\n![](images/14/146676b86f179710731d5e1ec1b4e5e5e3cf4cdf1af0d4cd4db3d709a36ea0c6.svg)\n\nAlso, since ![](images/c6/c6dbfaa7b99647ca21ec1ad2c57f563756ed17cec04b559d89a086571f1419a1.svg) is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n![](images/94/940eac51696aa93c499bd105cf5726bbed6eaac94eda297d0743af6cd00a67d3.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/a6/a69eca8aae37a4ecd6d3653c994b2bea69e37a8be6dcb00c4a5eeafbc58f1382.svg) we get\n![](images/f5/f56355c56683deba996e3f8cbdd8a9862cd7a9f6f570be9ab12cd4520748e7ec.svg)\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." :tutorial/title "Writing a Position Vector Using Known Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403 :tutorial/content "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that ![](images/80/80eb0b356da6e6a2315ea1466d34b914c6cad5e59ff07c4d061ea82b69ed9838.svg) and we are asked to find ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo find ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we want to match the expression\n![](images/52/52599a11cea864ea801b87d8ec0ea288252afa4265395d9fbb4a4dc2815e9e7e.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) be the origin, and let ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/a1/a14e933631f2b075c5a0bb4ab14fad2ca2790c3be2122b1fe8f4b7193fb505b2.svg) end at points ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/21/2109f0ae1a3679002930f1bf8bfa3490cf23c540ae6bbd50c093e5064327ab15.svg) as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is ![](images/bd/bdd7762354dbbdecb02ccf8284e0830f5421588bbb7fbf1bd9c3a9268a750649.svg) we first draw the vector ![](images/cb/cbbad9d9d0e183ac112cfdf946b99fd00718e0c6856d1a75564925b0e2760e85.svg) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/a2/a2b2f82b89941e4065ecf4c3bf612ab3d7e66fdb16c848dd2ce169480714e7ae.svg) Then\n\n![](images/3a/3a9a2959c3a256cb2651e0f00b0d7fe774f95aeee92289284bffb4c3b11b843a.svg)\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n![](images/f1/f1e5980924989b13362a77d776ecbd4b7567f99e30fe26eb57ee99d18fa1d464.svg)\n\nAlso, the vector ![](images/5f/5f1f2cecb3a462fc45e60ec717c2fde5b1aaba79448a58aae1a9d3023b21b7bb.svg) has length ![](images/f5/f551620762fb84e33026129595b42ef514082cc2e137b6762d75fd574d56383c.svg) but points in the opposite direction to ![](images/85/853e57b0bf9a8f7c16e59d37575ae23ac4028ed66b30720af9d7d49ab595c91b.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/4f/4fdd6b0f88ff6e5ea11ace72a5b61c24fe8cf6b1d85b4bceee599dde18471b5d.svg)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for ![](images/3c/3cce8321317b15087f61b778b47963fdff160e7df1e3ade0d33b040e10125994.svg) we get\n\n![](images/71/71d018c5f48d3afb47781ddff61419623e8c6d15856151398905494498254880.svg)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/57897701facaf26cf0826a2b6a12f408ae1acd8a5f4e78575a4457f66a156d9b.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e6/e6090f180976846ce7a2e6e8d696a0fa078a471c950de6188908528c881839fb.svg) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/79/792ff38b4658d1d4bbfbc57cbe93be95dd8d90b24559f601250eaa81becf8d24.svg) we get\n\n![](images/99/99914dd947bc5c5acbb4d9aa989efb9fef4dc78b0242629ea0508a81e7058f61.svg)" :tutorial/title "Vectors in Opposite Directions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1df1c2f113360cd3a34d8ef29df62e9.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/06/06046901b5ecb50bfedb17ffaf4c19cf690e50bd2e6e7c668fa59787ce4d71b7.png)\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/49/492123d15daf8306f0f5f893c7230d60f9eb8312d17b20f9a331f40f36b69b48.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/d8/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](images/2d/2d7c2df783579edf0ca151150eeb55471b3f3fc2afc4e7836678b25670b484c4.png)\n\nIn ![](images/3e/3e50bb6908d0311cbf9fe09b7a8039c9d438eeda7d312f7fa87d6aa835523d49.png) we have:\n\n- ![](images/9a/9a61fb0fb1ada64abe8bd22d8b6f14a8248212de90bff583ebd1881bdf21efc5.png)\n\n- ![](images/af/af03af84c1a16b4d534cc63ac9a1b3d8d5b7d181b32cb23190deb192d4d8b8b1.png) and ![](images/d9/d9dc9bcbe34586fe4e368536ed74377c765274e2e5b008e1fee5d65e3b5008c0.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da5024a9515dce187468fd87b63acfd0fd89bfd867a9f03388782221604a3e74.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](images/cb/cb4b9bd2f50fd4b2da78369b90c522bc2b7ee20cbafa9460b643889733487c5b.png)\n\n- ![](images/5f/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](images/1f/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da69cf085ea0816397356abf0878c798ab653c04fe34a51b8b849610d57778d4.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](images/57/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/578802d62c9ab4cfbc8f250975b5b7be1396907593ba95901b8b21ad6a116d04.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](images/1c/1c2480355dc4e29a136a3bd7b6855e076f1c9698507523cafbd934521b91c6bf.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487}}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096886}] [#edb/ref 17592186048749 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097413} :activity/id #uuid "64f2826e-10ec-5427-8fd0-5dacd740fef4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097813 :tutorial/content "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same. \n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096410 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \n                          f'(x)             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right)\\\n                                             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\n\t\t\t\t\t     &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\t\t\t\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  12x^{3} - 2x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097814 :tutorial/content "If we replace  the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" :tutorial/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097418}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097815 :tutorial/content "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097806 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the rule\n![](images/77/77334bed6aafc3383552d90bcfc8da614d56493edce4e7ab89a2f28e01d3dbfa.svg)\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of ![](images/5b/5ba79af546498192229d0cdd5a4a922d1d015802402b8eec5910fe00f999820d.svg) \n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n![](images/09/091c69568c8472aacf8229a756a835e0edc26700677e4fa67de3d2f6ac0d96a2.svg)\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n![](images/c9/c9b146d550ef068823a4ee4b9f63eea89f46d6ceecedf0ad4baa6462f11d9127.svg)\n\nNow, the following result can be used (we'll prove it in the subsequent topics):\n\n![](images/ce/cedfc7ab127ac8b34cd72b21e8c3e527541e054721324d9d948d6df123cd61e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/5880fb665abda7f3cef4ad7425c99c43b16e84c448c7b9e22ff32f76e36df87c.svg)\n\nas required." :tutorial/title "Justifying the Second Rule"}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096892}] [#edb/ref 17592186048678 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350} :activity/id #uuid "0a5bdaa9-fe87-516a-8c94-9bc96565f389" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139667 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n{x - y =-4; 6x - y = 6\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/ee/ee391c2910264abc2962d0c5ec3d971eb7b0b818b5bd59af33fc16ab9e931d11.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6)$.\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations.\n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -4 \\\\\n(2) - (6) &= -4 \\\\\n- 4 &= -4✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 6 \\\\\n6(2) - 6 &= 6 \\\\\n6 &= 6✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138083 :question/id #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13129" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n${x + 2y = 8; 2x - y = 6$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138084 :question/id #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13130" :question/problem "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days."} :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138586}]}
